#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179490 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "ccfb8833-f270-5855-8c49-fb3b6b6662d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-18776" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation $5^{x+2}=9^{x-2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $\\left(\\ln\\right)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x+2} & ={9}^{x-2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x+2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({9}^{x-2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(x+2)\\ln(5) = (x-2)\\ln(9).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =(x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)) & =2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)) \\\\ x & =\\frac{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9))}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179505 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e37891e-d24f-53cd-ba74-d887fa99aa3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions of Exponential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "453d4ec3-98f5-5db1-a046-a6b15c13049d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4097" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of $\\log_2,$ what value of $x$ satisfies the equation $2^{x+1}=3^x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take $\\log_2$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+1} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡({2}^{x+1}) & ={log}_{2}⁡({3}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\log_b \\left( b^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\log_2 \\left(3^{x}\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+1 = x \\log_2 \\left(3\\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x{log}_{2}⁡(3) \\\\ x{log}_{2}⁡(3)-x & =1 \\\\ x({log}_{2}⁡(3)-1) & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{{log}_{2}⁡(3)-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179506 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/id #uuid "fbb90aa1-5adf-5c6d-8499-68eea953cc42" :question/math-academy-id "e-4098" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation $e^{x+1}=2^x\\cdot 5?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $\\left(\\ln\\right)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+1} & ={2}^{x}\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x}\\cdot 5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\ln \\left( e^a \\right) = a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+1 =\\ln \\left(2^{x}\\cdot 5\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power and product laws of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x+1 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-1 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-1}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cae0871-1a20-54c5-b61a-0695c6255e8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566486} {:answer/id #uuid "7e61491a-edb7-59c9-a238-2402700b4b3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566603} {:answer/id #uuid "a8931b84-182c-5653-94fc-0a435f916fc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566694} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9ae13b-6f71-5bf8-8fe4-8034501753cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566705} {:answer/id #uuid "e09c8d64-8c16-57a8-9d83-3c0e974f4b8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e61491a-edb7-59c9-a238-2402700b4b3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566603} :answer-field/id #uuid "df525b75-5def-5db5-bf1c-dec1da3b1a21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "532b62bc-05d4-54bd-b88c-d3ad9a2bd774" :question/math-academy-id "q-100469" :question/problem "Find the solution of ${3}^{x+4}={6}^{2-3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x+4} & ={6}^{2-3x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{x+4}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{2-3x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567094 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e37891e-d24f-53cd-ba74-d887fa99aa3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions of Exponential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df69fe68-4c4e-539d-abe6-c6727d99a934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.412" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df69fe68-4c4e-539d-abe6-c6727d99a934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.412" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566811} :answer-field/id #uuid "db5c3cce-a76e-57c9-807c-d1d10889bec0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e11adf3d-e629-5f12-b77c-eadc477f80fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-233241" :question/problem "Given that ${3}^{x-2}={5}^{4x},$ what is the value of $x?$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take ${log}_{3}$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x-2} & ={5}^{4x} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{x-2}) & ={log}_{3}⁡({5}^{4x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nx-2={log}_{3}⁡({5}^{4x}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx-2=4x{log}_{3}⁡(5)\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for $x,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =4x{log}_{3}⁡(5) \\\\ x & =2+4x{log}_{3}⁡(5) \\\\ x-4x{log}_{3}⁡(5) & =2 \\\\ x(1-4{log}_{3}⁡(5)) & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{1-4{log}_{3}⁡(5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the change of base formula, we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡(5)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}≈1.464\\,974.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $x$ as\n\n$$\nx≈\\frac{2}{1-4(1.464\\,974)}≈-0.412,\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5012993-bc88-5829-a54b-d1ece2e35800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}(2)-2}{1-\\operatorname{ln}(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289335} {:answer/id #uuid "3c60bd8f-a706-531d-9399-e9f54908a43b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\ln 2-2}{1-\\ln 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c60bd8f-a706-531d-9399-e9f54908a43b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\ln 2-2}{1-\\ln 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566484} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f169737-4695-5e9f-9c2c-725e6eebea72" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f1f31343-a477-520d-bcc1-1e38225689fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-242902" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{x+2}={2}^{x+3}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+2} & ={2}^{x+3} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x+2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x+3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx+2=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x+3}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+2=(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x+2 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-2 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-2}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21d761ae-634f-5860-8997-6fdec80544c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}(3)-3\\operatorname{ln}(8)}{\\operatorname{ln}(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}(8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288248} {:answer/id #uuid "210d5c63-624a-5e84-b99a-4756e421309d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\ln 3-3\\ln 8}{\\ln 3+2\\ln 8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "210d5c63-624a-5e84-b99a-4756e421309d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\ln 3-3\\ln 8}{\\ln 3+2\\ln 8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566433} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ead1d1a-2134-5c1c-a34f-5c3f78e7a514" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1a98c1d-e002-56dc-98cf-733b76c221c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-242914" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${3}^{4-x}={8}^{2x+3}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4-x} & ={8}^{2x+3} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{4-x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({8}^{2x+3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(4-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)=(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ -x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ x & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dcd191d-3785-54a0-a639-e0094f61d3df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288129} {:answer/id #uuid "6905831a-aab1-5aa6-90c9-108a514050cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288770} {:answer/id #uuid "78f720c7-027b-52c2-b6ef-5787c36db04a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288863} {:answer/id #uuid "978813b7-39da-53a5-9479-68b97e87dbf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289046} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb5517e-f727-547d-b85c-617c8b602edd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "978813b7-39da-53a5-9479-68b97e87dbf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289046} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c40079c-8840-5be3-8c8b-803492714f39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "399f64e6-0f82-5ca3-861d-fbe99de11ba6" :question/math-academy-id "q-242923" :question/problem "Find the solution of ${3}^{2x}={8}^{1-6x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2x} & ={8}^{1-6x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({8}^{1-6x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(1-6x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(1-6x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)-6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8))}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335915 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "023d00f0-da47-5394-ae60-c245b8db80c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡3}{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333008} {:answer/id #uuid "6e0a0f48-9424-5652-a7e7-eb0fd57d5483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333989} {:answer/id #uuid "b328c212-2782-537f-811b-a0f14c00e543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334562} {:answer/id #uuid "c41479bc-c413-5965-9e33-9fcecff3e7ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334691} {:answer/id #uuid "c56d2556-b830-55ee-b819-6acd7ca640c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c41479bc-c413-5965-9e33-9fcecff3e7ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334691} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f4b2c7f-91fb-5e15-9837-4a5d33297bf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fbdd794-6943-5168-9b56-03ebeeb88547" :question/math-academy-id "q-66896" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation ${e}^{x-1}={3}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-1} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x-1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & =1 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b68103c-8572-5def-af47-c40ebf7a912d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333093} {:answer/id #uuid "1a07c5ce-d622-56e3-8b87-d3c6d18ac6cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333228} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8b1502-3196-5174-858b-a83b60796658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333537} {:answer/id #uuid "7300e6a7-8689-5691-ad3b-05bd9fed3149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334029} {:answer/id #uuid "79427cd3-1198-5fc8-b966-86d13c96fb8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a8b1502-3196-5174-858b-a83b60796658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333537} :answer-field/id #uuid "8709b860-c04b-54e2-b0a8-2dc526283008" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9213f98b-a1d4-51c9-9cab-9e4a798d60d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-66897" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation ${e}^{x-5}={4}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-5} & ={4}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x-5}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 & =5 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e37891e-d24f-53cd-ba74-d887fa99aa3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions of Exponential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f7025d6-e2e5-5fc5-8b47-e0a46d74075f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.995" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566428} {:answer/id #uuid "943a5115-a20e-5926-b36c-7168e5fdc7f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.630" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566653} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a9abea-fb40-5785-8a95-cf85401a4fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.710" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566778} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6f040e-36a1-5bf4-90ea-79902d46f926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.825" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566842} {:answer/id #uuid "f63f5618-89ac-54a2-870b-baafc4120d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.013" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2a9abea-fb40-5785-8a95-cf85401a4fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.710" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566778} :answer-field/id #uuid "41f866df-faa9-5b4e-afa9-25a8fe2253fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f054daca-f42a-55cb-aa6e-67968f5ef4ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-98908" :question/problem "Given that ${3}^{x}={2}^{x-1},$ what is the value of $x?$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we take ${log}_{3}$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x} & ={2}^{x-1} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{x}) & ={log}_{3}⁡({2}^{x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡({2}^{x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(x-1){log}_{3}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(x-1){log}_{3}⁡(2) \\\\ x & =x{log}_{3}⁡(2)-{log}_{3}⁡(2) \\\\ x-x{log}_{3}⁡(2) & =-{log}_{3}⁡(2) \\\\ x(1-{log}_{3}⁡(2)) & =-{log}_{3}⁡(2) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(2)}{1-{log}_{3}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the change of base formula, we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡(2)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}≈0.630\\,930.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $x$ as\n\n$$\nx≈-\\frac{0.630\\,930}{1-0.630\\,930}≈-1.710,\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}]}
